former Secretary of State Condoleezza  Rice was appointed national security  advisor by president george w bush in  2001 four years later she became the  first female african-american secretary  of state while in office she championed  an initiative called transformational  diplomacy it was intended to expand and  strengthen democratic institutions  worldwide rice drew from personal  experiences in her new book democracy  stories from the long road to freedom it  examines how democracies are fostered  and sometimes fail  she writes there is no more thrilling  moment than when people finally seize  their rights and their Liberty that  moment is necessary right and inevitable  it is also terrifying and disruptive and  chaotic and what follows it is hard  really really hard Condoleezza Rice  joins us now at this table good morning  good morning  I remember speech you made in Cairo and  what you basically said for 60 years my  country the United States pursued  stability at the expense of democracy in  this region here in the Middle East and  we achieved neither now we're taking a  different course we're supporting the  Democratic aspirations of all people yes  it's important to do so so how we doing  you're telling us stories in it yes well  as this with anything that's worth doing  it's hard and the jury's out for a very  long time but we've seen some successes  and we've seen some reverses and one of  the things that I wanted to do with this  book is to challenge those who keep  saying that democracy is in recession or  democracy is going backwards to remember  that this is a very long road and there  will be setbacks but for every turkey  with erawan or every Egypt where the  Arab Spring has given way to a military  coup you have a Colombia which has has  rescued itself from a failed state in  the early 2000s with American help you  have a Kenya that despite contested  elections continues along a more or less  democratic path and I wanted to remind  people also that our own history is one  of a very very long road to freedom an  American Constitution where my ancestors  were once counted as three  it's a man where my father couldn't vote  reliably in 1952 in Birmingham Alabama  and now we've had a black president  I've been Secretary of State Colin  Powell has been Secretary of State we  sometimes lose we lose sight of the big  picture you called slavery America's  birth defect which i think is a very  poetic way to describe I've never heard  it described that way well a birth  defect is something that is always with  you but it's always something also  something that you can overcome I think  in this case we see that this birth  defect of slavery colors the way that we  look at each other we are not a  colorblind society we probably never  will be  sometimes it causes us to look at each  other with suspicion and that's at the  root of any number of problems including  issues of policing that we've been  concerned with and so I like to describe  it in that way but I like to remind  people that every day we get up and we  try to make this imperfect democracy a  little bit better we try to include more  people in We the People and that's  America's story let me ask you this book  though you write Americans seem to be  experiencing a kind of crisis of  confidence about our own democracy who  we are and what we value yeah that is  true under President Trump well it's  been true for some time we've seen these  these polls for a long time that  Americans are losing confidence in the  institutions and that no institution in  America today is really fully trusted  and since as I write in this book  democracy is always at that knife's edge  between chaos and authoritarianism  democracy is the sweet spot where we  channel our concerns our our wants our  desires through institutions if you're  in favor democracy what do you as a  nation do for the aspirations of people  in other countries well the question yes  and it raises questions of sovereignty  and other issues  well sovereignty should never be a  defense or tyranny and for mistreating  your people I think first of all you pay  attention to us here I'm a big believer  that America has to be strong at home  America  have to feel confident but we have  learned I think the hard way that when  we don't champion the rights of people  bad things happen so no one is unhappy  that these days that Germany and Japan  wants states that challenge their  neighbors that were violent in their  regions are now democracies and our  pillars of the International people are  unhappy about Egypt for example  yes but Egypt under Mubarak one of the  things we want to do charlie is we want  to make sure that when we have friends  and allies of course we're going to have  to talk to the Egyptian president but  just think had Mubarak reformed maybe  there wouldn't have been a revolution  condi you write in your book about  meeting Vladimir Putin for the first  time you even say that you felt sorry  for him well I felt bad because I met  him in in 1992 he was deputy mayor of  st. Petersburg he was standing at this  very fancy party and he was there in a  kind of Soviet era that's all alone in a  great a pale short well I was pale and  sore but boy does he have piercing eyes  I will tell you that and now you see him  as what is there any doubt in your mind  about the role that Vladimir Putin has  played in our country I see him as an  authoritarian with an assertive and  aggressive foreign policy I see him as  somebody who is engaged in trying to  subvert democracy in a number of  countries and I do see him as someone  who undoubtedly interfered in our  election but he's an eye-for-an-eye kind  of guy  in 2012 we said his election was  fraudulent he's trying to say to us let  me show you that yours can be too and we  have to be careful we should investigate  I'm furious at the Russians for what  they've done but let's not give him the  satisfaction of thinking that he has  caused Americans to doubt the legitimacy  of our own democratic processes that  would help him to win Condoleezza Rice  thank you and her book democracy stories  from the long road to freedom goes on  sale tomorrow
 

